        <front>
            <dhq:abstract>
                <!-- Include a brief abstract of the article -->
                <p>Topic models are highly sensitive to corpus construction, preprocessing, stopword
                    removal, segmentation, model implementation, and the selected number of topics.
                    Yet rather than present these limitations as something new, this article
                    revisits topic modeling to ask what standards of transparency and
                    reproducibility should follow from them. Through a case study of Fernando
                    Pessoa’s <title rend="italic">Livro do Desassossego</title> (<title
                        rend="italic">The Book of Disquiet</title>), we compare topic modeling
                    outputs across different editorial versions, preprocessing conditions, and
                    modeling configurations. Using Quanteda for corpus preparation and
                    document–feature matrix construction, and seededlda for unsupervised and guided
                    topic modeling, we show how apparently technical decisions shape outcomes. The
                    article argues that topic modeling should be understood as an exploratory
                    interpretive procedure rather than as a neutral mechanism for discovering
                    themes. Its scholarly value depends on making the full modeling pipeline — from
                    source acquisition and cleaning to parameter selection, code, derived data, and
                    interpretation — available for inspection, reuse, and critical contestation.</p>
            </dhq:abstract>
